LP.section({"id": "roles", "title": "Career Paths", "icon": "🧭", "blurb": "DE vs AI DE vs GenAI vs LLM vs ML vs DS vs DA — finally clear."});
LP.page({"id": "roles-overview", "section": "roles", "title": "The whole map of data & AI jobs", "level": "Zero", "minutes": 9, "tags": ["careers", "must-know"], "body": "\nThe titles in this field are a mess. Companies use the same word to mean different things. This page gives you the clean mental model so you never feel lost in a job description again.\n\n<div class=\"widget-mount\" data-widget=\"roleExplorer\"><\/div>\n\n## The one-line version of each role\n\n| Role | In one line | Core question they answer |\n|------|-------------|---------------------------|\n| **Data Analyst (DA)** | Explains what *already happened* using charts & SQL. | \"What were last month's sales?\" |\n| **Data Scientist (DS)** | Builds models to *predict* and find deep insights. | \"Which customers will churn next month?\" |\n| **Data Engineer (DE)** | Builds the *pipes* that move & shape data reliably. | \"How do we get clean data to everyone, at scale?\" |\n| **AI / ML Engineer** | Puts ML models into *production* software. | \"How do we serve this model to a million users?\" |\n| **AI Data Engineer** | A DE who also feeds the *AI/ML systems* (features, vectors). | \"How do we pipeline data *for AI* reliably?\" |\n| **GenAI Engineer** | Builds apps on top of generative models (RAG, agents). | \"How do we build a chatbot over our company docs?\" |\n| **LLM Engineer** | Specialises in language models: fine-tuning, serving, evals. | \"How do we train/optimise/deploy this LLM?\" |\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-note\"><span class=\"cfor\">🧠<\/span><div><strong>The key split:<\/strong> Analysts &amp; Scientists mostly <em>use<\/em> data to produce insight. Engineers <em>build the systems<\/em> that make data and models usable, fast, and reliable at scale. This academy focuses on the <strong>engineering<\/strong> side — the roles in highest demand and least crowded.<\/div><\/div>\n\n## Two axes that explain everything\n\nPlot any role on two questions:\n\n1. **Do you build systems, or produce insights?** (Engineering vs Analysis/Science)\n2. **Do you work with classic data, or AI/models?** (Data platform vs AI/ML)\n\n<div class=\"quadrant\" aria-label=\"Roles plotted on two axes: build vs insight, and classic data vs AI\">\n  <span class=\"q-ax q-ax-t\">Build systems · engineering<\/span>\n  <span class=\"q-ax q-ax-b\">Produce insights · analysis &amp; science<\/span>\n  <span class=\"q-ax q-ax-l\">Classic data<\/span>\n  <span class=\"q-ax q-ax-r\">AI / models<\/span>\n  <div class=\"q-cell\"><strong>Data Engineer<\/strong><span>pipelines · warehouses<\/span><\/div>\n  <div class=\"q-cell\"><strong>AI Data · ML · GenAI · LLM Engineer<\/strong><span>data &amp; models in production<\/span><\/div>\n  <div class=\"q-cell\"><strong>Data Analyst<\/strong><span>dashboards · reporting<\/span><\/div>\n  <div class=\"q-cell\"><strong>Data Scientist<\/strong><span>statistics · experiments<\/span><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n## Which should *you* aim for?\n\n- **Love clean structure, logic, building reliable things?** → **Data Engineer** (best entry point; huge demand; clear path).\n- **Already a DE and want to ride the AI wave?** → **AI Data Engineer** then **GenAI/LLM Engineer**.\n- **Love maths, statistics, experiments?** → **Data Scientist**.\n- **Love software engineering + AI?** → **ML / AI Engineer**.\n- **Want to build cool AI apps fast?** → **GenAI Engineer** (most beginner-friendly *AI* role today).\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-tip\"><span class=\"cfor\">💡<\/span><div><strong>Honest advice for beginners:<\/strong> Start by becoming a solid <strong>Data Engineer<\/strong>. It's the most learnable from zero, the most in demand, and it is the foundation for <em>every<\/em> AI role. You can specialise into GenAI/LLM later — and you'll be far better at it because you'll understand the data underneath.<\/div><\/div>\n\nThe next pages break down each role in detail: what you do day-to-day, what to learn, the salary range, and how to get in.\n"});
LP.page({"id": "role-data-engineer", "section": "roles", "title": "Data Engineer — the foundation role", "level": "Beginner", "minutes": 9, "tags": ["careers", "data engineer"], "body": "\nThe **Data Engineer (DE)** builds and maintains the systems that collect, store, and transform data so that analysts, scientists, and AI models can use it. If data is water, the DE builds the plumbing.\n\n## What you actually do day-to-day\n- Write **pipelines** that move data from sources (apps, APIs, files) into storage.\n- Clean, join, and reshape data with **SQL** and **Python/PySpark**.\n- Design **data models** (how tables relate) and build **data warehouses**.\n- Schedule and monitor jobs with **orchestration** tools (Airflow).\n- Make pipelines **reliable, fast, and trustworthy** (data quality, testing, alerting).\n- Manage cost and performance on the **cloud**.\n\n## The skill stack\n| Layer | Tools you'll learn here |\n|-------|-------------------------|\n| Language | **Python**, **SQL** (non-negotiable) |\n| Big data | **Apache Spark / PySpark** |\n| Storage | Data lakes (S3/ADLS/GCS), warehouses (**Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift**) |\n| Pipelines | **Airflow**, dbt, batch + **streaming (Kafka)** |\n| Cloud | One of **AWS / Azure / GCP** |\n| Modelling | Dimensional modelling, **SCD**, normalization |\n| Foundations | Linux basics, Git, **HLD/LLD** (system design) |\n\n## Why it's the best entry point\n\n<div class=\"feat-grid\">\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"terminal\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>Learnable from zero<\/strong><p>Mostly logical code-and-SQL skills, less heavy maths than data science.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"chart\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>Massive, durable demand<\/strong><p>Every company has data; few can engineer it well.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"route\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>Clear ladder<\/strong><p>Junior to DE to Senior to Lead/Architect, or pivot into AI roles.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"sparkles\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>Foundation for AI<\/strong><p>Every GenAI/LLM system needs a data engineer behind it.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n## Rough salary signal\nAmong the highest-paid non-managerial tech roles. In India, mid-level DEs often out-earn equivalent web developers; in the US, senior DEs commonly reach the top tech pay bands. *(Numbers vary wildly by country, company, and year — treat as direction, not promise.)*\n\n## How to get in (summary — full plan in Roadmaps)\n\n<nav class=\"pathway pathway-flow\"><span class=\"pw-step\"><span class=\"pw-n\">1<\/span>SQL + Python<\/span><span class=\"pw-arrow\"><i data-ic=\"arrowRight\" class=\"ic-sm\"><\/i><\/span><span class=\"pw-step\"><span class=\"pw-n\">2<\/span>Cloud + warehouse + Spark + Airflow<\/span><span class=\"pw-arrow\"><i data-ic=\"arrowRight\" class=\"ic-sm\"><\/i><\/span><span class=\"pw-step\"><span class=\"pw-n\">3<\/span>Portfolio projects<\/span><span class=\"pw-arrow\"><i data-ic=\"arrowRight\" class=\"ic-sm\"><\/i><\/span><span class=\"pw-step\"><span class=\"pw-n\">4<\/span>Data modelling + system design<\/span><span class=\"pw-arrow\"><i data-ic=\"arrowRight\" class=\"ic-sm\"><\/i><\/span><span class=\"pw-step\"><span class=\"pw-n\">5<\/span>Interview Bank<\/span><\/nav>\n\n1. Master **SQL** + **Python**.\n2. Learn **one cloud** + a **warehouse** + **Spark** + **Airflow**.\n3. Build **2–3 portfolio projects** (an end-to-end pipeline is gold).\n4. Learn **data modelling** and **system design** for interviews.\n5. Drill the **Interview Bank** here, especially SQL and pipeline design.\n\n> **Key takeaway:** The DE builds reliable data pipelines and warehouses. It's the most beginner-accessible, in-demand role and the launchpad for everything AI.\n"});
LP.page({"id": "role-ai-data-engineer", "section": "roles", "title": "AI Data Engineer — DE for the AI era", "level": "Intermediate", "minutes": 8, "tags": ["careers", "ai", "data engineer"], "body": "\nAn **AI Data Engineer** is a data engineer whose pipelines feed **machine-learning and AI systems** rather than only dashboards. The plumbing is similar, but the \"taps\" are models, feature stores, and vector databases instead of reports.\n\n## What's different from a normal DE?\nA normal DE delivers clean tables for humans/BI. An **AI Data Engineer** additionally delivers:\n\n<div class=\"feat-grid\">\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"layers\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>Features<\/strong><p>Builds and maintains a feature store that serves the same computed features for both training and live prediction.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"database\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>Embeddings & vector data<\/strong><p>Turns text/images into vectors and loads them into vector databases for GenAI search and RAG.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"cube\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>Training datasets<\/strong><p>Large, clean, well-labelled, reproducible datasets for model training and fine-tuning.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"zap\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>Real-time data<\/strong><p>Low-latency streaming for live model inference.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"target\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>ML-aware data quality<\/strong><p>Watches for data drift, when incoming data slowly changes and silently breaks a model.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n## Extra skills on top of the DE stack\n| Area | What to add |\n|------|-------------|\n| ML basics | Train/validation/test, features, what a model needs |\n| Vectors | Embeddings, vector DBs (**FAISS, pgvector, Pinecone, Milvus**) |\n| Feature stores | **Feast**, Databricks/Vertex feature stores |\n| MLOps-adjacent | Experiment tracking (**MLflow**), data/version control (**DVC, lakeFS**) |\n| Pipelines for AI | Orchestrating training + inference data flows |\n\n## Why this role is exploding\nEvery company adopting AI discovers the same painful truth: **the model is the easy part; the data pipeline is the hard part.** Someone has to reliably get clean, fresh, well-shaped data into and out of models. That someone is the AI Data Engineer — and there are far too few of them.\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-tip\"><span class=\"cfor\">💡<\/span><div><strong>Career hack:<\/strong> Become a strong Data Engineer first, then add the AI-specific layer (vectors, feature stores, ML basics). You instantly become rarer and more valuable than both pure DEs and pure data scientists.<\/div><\/div>\n\n> **Key takeaway:** AI Data Engineer = Data Engineer + the data plumbing AI needs (features, embeddings, vector stores, training data, drift monitoring). One of the fastest-growing, least-crowded roles.\n"});
LP.page({"id": "role-genai-llm-engineer", "section": "roles", "title": "GenAI Engineer vs LLM Engineer", "level": "Intermediate", "minutes": 9, "tags": ["careers", "genai", "llm"], "body": "\nThese two titles overlap a lot and many companies blur them. Here's the cleanest way to separate them.\n\n## GenAI Engineer — builds *applications* on top of models\nA **GenAI (Generative AI) Engineer** is, at heart, a **software engineer who builds products powered by generative models** — usually by *calling* existing models (Claude, GPT, etc.) rather than training them.\n\nTypical work:\n\n<div class=\"feat-grid\">\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"database\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>RAG systems<\/strong><p>A chatbot that answers from your company's documents. You'll build RAG hands-on in the GenAI module.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"message\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>Prompt engineering<\/strong><p>Designing the instructions you give the model.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"route\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>AI agents<\/strong><p>Models that use tools, call APIs, and take multi-step actions.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"layers\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>Integration<\/strong><p>Embeddings and vector search, plus managing cost, latency, and safety.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n  <div class=\"feat\"><span class=\"feat-ic\"><i data-ic=\"check\"><\/i><\/span><div><strong>Evals<\/strong><p>Measure output quality and reduce hallucinations (when a model confidently makes things up).<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\nSkills: Python, API integration, vector databases, frameworks like **LangChain / LlamaIndex**, prompt design, basic web/backend.\n\n## LLM Engineer — works *deeper* on the models themselves\nAn **LLM Engineer** works one layer down, closer to the model's internals:\n- **Fine-tuning** models on custom data (full fine-tuning or efficient methods like **LoRA / QLoRA**).\n- **Serving & optimising** models: quantization, batching, GPU inference, tools like **vLLM**.\n- Building **evaluation** harnesses and benchmarks.\n- Sometimes **pre-training** or continued-training of open models (**Llama, Mistral**).\n- Understanding the **transformer architecture**, tokenization, context windows, attention.\n\nSkills: strong Python + **PyTorch**, the Hugging Face stack (**transformers, datasets, PEFT**), GPUs, distributed training (e.g. **Ray, DeepSpeed**), deep ML fundamentals.\n\n## Side-by-side\n\n| | GenAI Engineer | LLM Engineer |\n|---|----------------|--------------|\n| Main job | Build apps that *use* models | Build/optimise the *models* |\n| Depth | App + integration layer | Model internals + infra |\n| Trains models? | Rarely (mostly calls APIs) | Yes (fine-tunes, sometimes pre-trains) |\n| Core tools | LangChain, vector DBs, prompts | PyTorch, Hugging Face, vLLM, GPUs |\n| Maths needed | Light | Heavier (deep learning) |\n| Easiest entry? | **Yes** — friendliest AI role for beginners | Harder — needs ML depth |\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-note\"><span class=\"cfor\">🧭<\/span><div><strong>Which to target?<\/strong> If you want to build AI products quickly and you're newer, aim for <strong>GenAI Engineer<\/strong>. If you love the deep ML/maths and infrastructure, and want to shape models themselves, aim for <strong>LLM Engineer<\/strong>. Both pay extremely well and both rest on solid data-engineering foundations.<\/div><\/div>\n\n> **Key takeaway:** GenAI Engineer = builds *applications* using generative models (RAG, agents, prompts). LLM Engineer = works on the *models* themselves (fine-tuning, serving, evals, architecture). GenAI is the easier on-ramp; LLM is the deeper specialisation.\n"});
LP.page({"id": "role-comparison-table", "section": "roles", "title": "Master comparison & how they work together", "level": "Beginner", "minutes": 7, "tags": ["careers", "must-know"], "body": "\nKeep this page bookmarked. It's the cheat-sheet for the whole career landscape.\n\n## The full comparison\n\n| Role | Builds or analyses? | Main tools | Maths load | Best for someone who… |\n|------|--------------------|-----------|------------|------------------------|\n| **Data Analyst** | Analyses | SQL, Excel, Power BI/Tableau | Low | likes answering business questions with charts |\n| **Data Scientist** | Analyses/Models | Python, stats, scikit-learn, notebooks | High | loves maths, statistics, experiments |\n| **Data Engineer** | Builds | SQL, Python, Spark, Airflow, cloud | Low–Med | loves logic, structure, reliable systems |\n| **AI Data Engineer** | Builds | DE stack + vectors, feature stores, MLflow | Medium | wants DE skills aimed at AI |\n| **ML Engineer** | Builds | Python, PyTorch/TF, MLOps, Docker/K8s | High | loves software engineering + ML |\n| **GenAI Engineer** | Builds | LangChain, vector DBs, prompts, APIs | Low–Med | wants to ship AI products fast |\n| **LLM Engineer** | Builds | PyTorch, HF transformers, GPUs, vLLM | High | wants to go deep on the models |\n\n## How they collaborate on one real project\nImagine a bank building an **AI assistant that answers customer questions from policy documents**. The work flows down the chain, each role handing off to the next:\n\n<nav class=\"pathway pathway-flow\"><span class=\"pw-step\"><span class=\"pw-n\">1<\/span>Data Engineer<\/span><span class=\"pw-arrow\"><i data-ic=\"arrowRight\" class=\"ic-sm\"><\/i><\/span><span class=\"pw-step\"><span class=\"pw-n\">2<\/span>AI Data Engineer<\/span><span class=\"pw-arrow\"><i data-ic=\"arrowRight\" class=\"ic-sm\"><\/i><\/span><span class=\"pw-step\"><span class=\"pw-n\">3<\/span>GenAI Engineer<\/span><span class=\"pw-arrow\"><i data-ic=\"arrowRight\" class=\"ic-sm\"><\/i><\/span><span class=\"pw-step\"><span class=\"pw-n\">4<\/span>LLM Engineer<\/span><span class=\"pw-arrow\"><i data-ic=\"arrowRight\" class=\"ic-sm\"><\/i><\/span><span class=\"pw-step\"><span class=\"pw-n\">5<\/span>Data Scientist / Analyst<\/span><\/nav>\n\n1. **Data Engineer** builds pipelines that collect and clean all the policy PDFs and customer data into a lake/warehouse.\n2. **AI Data Engineer** converts documents into **embeddings** and loads them into a **vector database**; sets up the feature/data flow.\n3. **GenAI Engineer** builds the **RAG chatbot**: retrieves the right document chunks and prompts an LLM to answer.\n4. **LLM Engineer** **fine-tunes** the model on the bank's tone and optimises it to run cheaply and fast.\n5. **Data Scientist / Analyst** measures whether it actually helps customers and reduces call-centre load.\n\nEveryone depends on the data engineer's pipes being solid. That's why we start there.\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-tip\"><span class=\"cfor\">💡<\/span><div><strong>Don't agonise over the \"perfect\" title.<\/strong> Skills overlap massively. Build a strong foundation (SQL, Python, pipelines), pick a direction, and let your real interests pull you toward a specialisation. Job titles are fuzzy; <em>skills<\/em> are what get you hired.<\/div><\/div>\n\n> **Key takeaway:** All these roles are points on a spectrum of *build vs analyse* and *data vs AI*. They collaborate on real projects, and they all stand on a data-engineering foundation. Now go pick your **Roadmap**.\n"});
